WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.920
Alright, we're going to start out with crutches canes and walkers.

2
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.320
On page 17.

3
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.320
Any questions?

4
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.000
Probably just went all the way to the left.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Yeah, we did.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.240
Not much canes in mind, okay.

7
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.240
Crutches canes and walkers.

8
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.320
One of the major areas of human functioning is locomotion.

9
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.560
And so they do test it.

10
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.800
So certain things show up on boards with greater frequency than what you might expect them

11
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.480
to.

12
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.800
And locomotion is one of those.

13
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:54.400
And so to test that, they test casts, traction, canes, crutches, walkers, things that were

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.980
really probably not emphasized in your school much, were they?

15
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.420
That wasn't a major emphasis, I doubt.

16
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.980
But on boards, people will always say, like, well, I was surprised at how many things I

17
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.300
had about traction or about casts or about things like that.

18
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:16.680
And that's because one of the eight areas of human functioning, they test its locomotion.

19
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.060
So you do have to know some of these things.

20
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:24.180
Plus, there are also really good areas for patient teaching because a lot of boards is

21
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.180
teaching.

22
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.180
What do you teach?

23
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.180
What's your goal?

24
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.980
What's the teaching?

25
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:36.540
And then it's also risk reduction, you know, as far as reducing risk to skin, risk for

26
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.080
immobility problems and that.

27
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.900
So let's talk about crutches.

28
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.300
The first thing is, is how do you measure crutches?

29
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.220
The reason why they want you to know how to measure crutches correctly is for risk reduction

30
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.460
so that you don't cause nerve damage.

31
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.420
The first thing is the length of the crutch.

32
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.100
How do you measure the length of the crutch?

33
00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:04.220
These two to three finger widths below the anterior axillary fold to a point lateral

34
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.940
to and slightly in front of the foot.

35
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:15.060
Now what they'll do is they'll say something like, you know, you see an RN or an LPN or

36
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.900
an A measuring a person for crutches.

37
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.300
You notice they're doing all of the following.

38
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.300
What would you correct?

39
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.660
And you have to pick something where they're doing it wrong.

40
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.180
Or they'll say which one shows the right and you have to pick out the correct.

41
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:34.020
The deal is, is they'll often write sets of instructions where you're measuring from the

42
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.820
wrong point to the wrong point.

43
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:42.100
For example, they'll say to get the length of a crutch, you measure from the axilla.

44
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.700
You don't measure from the axilla.

45
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:49.300
You measure two to three finger widths below the anterior axillary fold.

46
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.700
So they'll give you the wrong upper point.

47
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.940
Or they'll say you measure to the heel.

48
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.340
You don't measure to the heel.

49
00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.620
You measure to the little toe.

50
00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.020
You don't measure to the little toe.

51
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.420
In other words, you don't measure to any landmark on the foot.

52
00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:07.060
You measure to a point lateral to and slightly in front of the foot.

53
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:14.660
So if any of the answers say axilla or any landmark on your foot, they are incorrect.

54
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.940
They are wrong.

55
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.200
Rule them out.

56
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.140
So any instructions with a landmark on the foot or say axilla, they're wrong instructions.

57
00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.740
For how long the crutches need to be.

58
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.740
Letter B, the next thing you have to measure is the hand grip.

59
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.540
The hand grip can be adjusted up and down.

60
00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:40.300
And what you need to know there is when the hand grips are properly placed, the angle of

61
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.140
elbow flexion will be about 30 degrees.

62
00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:51.140
So those are the two things that they're all about with crutch measuring.

63
00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:58.140
The length and the angle of elbow flexion when you put the hand grip properly placed.

64
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.660
Those are the only two things you need to know about measuring crutches.

65
00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.740
The next part of it is how to teach crutch gates.

66
00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:09.500
Because you're supposed to be able to educate people on how to walk with crutches.

67
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.140
There are four crutch gates.

68
00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:15.340
Two point, three point, four point and swing through.

69
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.260
Now how do you teach each of them?

70
00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.500
Two point gate is rather simple.

71
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.180
These names are really obvious.

72
00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:26.860
You shouldn't forget these names because these names tell you exactly what you're doing with

73
00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.980
one or two, with one exception maybe.

74
00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:34.020
In two point gate, pretend my arms are crutches, alright?

75
00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.660
In two point gate you move a crutch and the opposite foot together.

76
00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.740
Followed by the other crutch and the other foot together.

77
00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.900
And so you kind of walk like this.

78
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:50.020
Two things, two things, two things, two things, two things.

79
00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:55.580
So it goes two, two, two, two, two.

80
00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:01.020
Well if you were going two, two, two, two, two, what would you name the gate?

81
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.260
Two point, does that make sense?

82
00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:06.340
Because in two point you're moving two things together.

83
00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.340
What two things?

84
00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.900
A crutch and the opposite.

85
00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.500
It's always crutch and opposite.

86
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.140
So that's real simple to teach.

87
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:18.980
In three point gate you're moving two crutches and the bad leg together.

88
00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.460
Two crutches and the bad leg.

89
00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.220
So how many things is that?

90
00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.220
Three.

91
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.220
Three.

92
00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:34.540
So it goes, if this is my bad leg, it goes three, one, three, one, three, one.

93
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:41.620
So what would you call a crutch gate that goes three, one, three, one, three, one?

94
00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.860
Three point, seems logical.

95
00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.620
So what are you moving together in two point?

96
00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.380
How many are you moving together in two point?

97
00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.380
Two.

98
00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.380
How many are you moving together in three point?

99
00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:52.380
Three.

100
00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.380
So how many do you move together in four point?

101
00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.380
Four.

102
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:56.380
No.

103
00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.380
Because that would be, what would that look like?

104
00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.380
That would look like this.

105
00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:06.540
And if you could do that, you don't need crutches.

106
00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.940
So obviously that falls apart.

107
00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.420
So you move two things together in two point, you move three things together in three point,

108
00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:18.180
but in four point, you move everything separately.

109
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.180
Because how many things do you have?

110
00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.500
Well, you have two legs and two crutches.

111
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.020
Two plus two equals four.

112
00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:28.980
So how does four point go?

113
00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.260
Well four point goes like this.

114
00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.580
You move a crutch, any crutch, just pick a crutch.

115
00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.500
But once you move that crutch, now you're locked in.

116
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.580
Your sequence is now locked in.

117
00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.140
Because now what must you move?

118
00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:44.660
The opposite foot followed by the other crutch followed by the other foot.

119
00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.380
So one, two, three, four.

120
00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.700
One, two, three, four.

121
00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.780
That's four point.

122
00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:57.140
It's very slow, but very stable.

123
00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.660
We'll be talking about that in the next page because that's exactly the next thing we need

124
00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:01.660
to tell you.

125
00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:04.140
Because you do have to know that.

126
00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.140
Okay.

127
00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:12.380
Swing through, on the other hand, is for non-weight bearing.

128
00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:18.300
For example, amputations where you can't bear weight on the stomach.

129
00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.860
Or whenever they say non-weight bearing.

130
00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.740
What swing through is, is you've seen it.

131
00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.860
I can't illustrate it because I don't really have crutches.

132
00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.540
But it's where you can't bear weight on this leg.

133
00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.940
And so you plant the crutches, right?

134
00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.980
And then you swing through.

135
00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:37.980
You see?

136
00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.700
And then you just, and you never put this leg down.

137
00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.180
If you've seen them, they probably go, who did they mention who?

138
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.860
And they just follow them.

139
00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:45.860
That's exactly what they do.

140
00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:46.860
And they just swing.

141
00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.020
And it actually can be really fast.

142
00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:50.420
Have you seen people go pretty fast with this?

143
00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.420
They just move.

144
00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.380
And that leg never touches down.

145
00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.580
Okay.

146
00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.420
So whether you swing through, non-weight bearing.

147
00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.060
I did have a student who called me after she took her test.

148
00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.860
And she says, that crutched stuff was on there, but what you taught me didn't help.

149
00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:09.860
Oh, okay.

150
00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.740
Rather accusatory, but hey, whatever.

151
00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.620
And I said, well, why didn't it help?

152
00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:20.380
And she said, well, you were telling us about the different crutch gates.

153
00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:26.260
And she said, the one that gave me nothing you taught us helped.

154
00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.940
And I said, well, what did they tell you?

155
00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.820
She said, well, he had an amputation.

156
00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:36.300
So I didn't know which crutch gate to use.

157
00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.220
And I said, well, what do you mean you didn't know?

158
00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:42.220
She says, well, two point wouldn't work.

159
00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.220
Three point would.

160
00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:47.500
She says, and then swing throughs for non-weight bearing.

161
00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.740
And I said, well, amputation, you can't bear.

162
00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.380
Do you see what I'm saying?

163
00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.260
Why do people make those kinds of mistakes on tests?

164
00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.260
They're not thinking.

165
00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.260
Why are they not thinking?

166
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.380
They're anxious and they're not.

167
00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.140
They're not taking steps to manage that anxiety.

168
00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.780
Now I will never tell a student when you take this test, don't be anxious.

169
00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.820
That's stupid.

170
00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:17.420
You can't tell a person not to be anxious because they're going to be anxious as

171
00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.300
whatever they're going to be.

172
00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:24.620
But you have to teach them is how to what manage the anxiety.

173
00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.220
And she wasn't managing the anxiety.

174
00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.220
And I was thinking, you know, but if you think about it, could you really do three

175
00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:30.220
point?

176
00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.220
You know, one, two, three.

177
00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:35.220
You can't do that with an amputation.

178
00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.220
You have to do swing through.

179
00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:41.220
Now what if they said they had an amputation with a prosthetic device?

180
00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.220
Could you bear weight?

181
00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:46.220
Yeah.

182
00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.220
Okay.

183
00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:55.220
Letter D on the next page, which is what you asked, which is when do they use these?

184
00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.220
In what situations do they use these?

185
00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.220
You also have to know that.

186
00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:05.220
Now this is easy because there's a little saying we often use and that's even for even,

187
00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:09.220
odd for odd, even for even, odd for odd.

188
00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.220
Now how does that work?

189
00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.220
Well, let's plug it in.

190
00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.220
Letter D number one.

191
00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:22.220
Use the even numbered gates, which are what?

192
00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.220
Two and four, correct?

193
00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:32.220
When the weakness is evenly distributed, that's even for even.

194
00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:37.220
Meaning use an even numbered gate when you have an even number of legs messed up.

195
00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:47.220
Use two point for a mild problem and four point for a severe problem.

196
00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:55.220
So generally answering your question, when do you use four point with severe bilateral weaknesses?

197
00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.220
When do you use a two point gate?

198
00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:06.220
Mild bilateral weaknesses.

199
00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:14.220
Because if you see, what my point is this is if they give you a question about what crutch gate will you teach?

200
00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:20.220
All you have to do to get it right is ask yourself how many legs are affected.

201
00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.220
If it's an even number of legs, which would be what?

202
00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:31.220
Two, then pick two or four, even for even.

203
00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:40.220
Okay, D number two here, use the odd numbered gate and there's only one odd numbered gate and that is three.

204
00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.220
When one leg is odd, that's the odd for odd.

205
00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:52.220
So if you say how many legs are affected? One. Well, what kind of a number is one, even or odd?

206
00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.220
Odd.

207
00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:57.220
Odd, so pick the odd numbered gate, which is three.

208
00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:01.220
But if you have two legs affected, is that an even or an odd number?

209
00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:05.220
Even, so pick an even numbered gate, two or four.

210
00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:09.220
Two for a mild or for a severe.

211
00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:17.220
And if they can't bear weight, you go swing through and if they're in amputation, you go swing through.

212
00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:22.220
So I'm going to give you some examples and I want you to tell me which crutch gate you would teach, alright?

213
00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:28.220
And I just want you to write it down.

214
00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:29.220
Number one.

215
00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:36.220
Early stages of rheumatoid arthritis.

216
00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:40.220
Early stages of rheumatoid arthritis.

217
00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:51.220
Left above the knee amputation.

218
00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:03.220
First day post-op, right knee replacement.

219
00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:07.220
Partial weight bearing allowed.

220
00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:18.220
Right or left? Did I say left or right?

221
00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:23.220
Right total knee replacement, first day post-op, partial weight bearing allowed.

222
00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:29.220
Advanced stages of amiotrophic lateral sclerosis, ALS.

223
00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.220
Advanced ALS.

224
00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:42.220
Left hip replacement, second day post-op,

225
00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:47.220
lateral sclerosis,

226
00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:54.220
left hip replacement, second day post-op, non-weight bearing.

227
00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:05.220
And just write down the crutch gate you're going to teach.

228
00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:09.220
Next one.

229
00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:15.220
Bilateral total knee replacement, bilateral total knee replacement.

230
00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:21.220
First day post-op, weight bearing allowed.

231
00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:29.220
Bilateral total knee replacement, first day post-op, weight bearing allowed.

232
00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.220
And last patient.

233
00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.220
Bilateral total knee replacement.

234
00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:45.220
Three weeks post-op.

235
00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:49.220
Okay.

236
00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:00.220
So do you have your crutch gates down?

237
00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:02.220
Okay, what's it read?

238
00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:05.220
What's the first one?

239
00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:09.220
Two. Why two?

240
00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.220
Did I tell you how many legs were affected?

241
00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.220
No, but it was a systemic disease.

242
00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.220
And what should you assume is a systemic disease?

243
00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:21.220
Both legs.

244
00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.220
So that's a, is to an even or odd number?

245
00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:28.220
Even. So you had to pick two or four. Why did you pick two?

246
00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.220
Early. Okay, then what was the next one?

247
00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:35.220
Swing through. Then what?

248
00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:37.220
Three.

249
00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.220
Then.

250
00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:41.220
Which one?

