489
00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:53.220
Okay, would you pick decreased energy level?

490
00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:59.220
Psychomotor retardation, meaning everything kind of slows down.

491
00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:04.220
Delusions of persecution.

492
00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:05.220
No.

493
00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.220
No. That would be totally out the window.

494
00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:10.220
Why? Because she is non-psychotic, and non-psychotics don't have hallucinations.

495
00:33:10.220 --> 00:33:13.220
And non-psychotics don't have delusions, hallucinations, and illusions.

496
00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:17.220
So you could automatically rule that out.

497
00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:23.220
Now, if they are psychotic, you rule those in, because they do have those.

498
00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:29.220
So let's talk about these psychotic symptoms in depth, because I'm going to teach you what they are and then how to treat them.

499
00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:33.220
The first psychotic symptom is a delusion.

500
00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:42.220
A delusion is a false, fixed, F-I-X-E-D, fixed, which means they don't change it.

501
00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:48.220
A false, fixed idea or belief.

502
00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:52.220
There is no sensory component.

503
00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:56.220
With a delusion, you're not hearing, feeling, tasting, seeing, smelling, anything.

504
00:33:56.220 --> 00:34:00.220
It's all up here. You're thinking it.

505
00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:05.220
You're not feeling it. You're not sensing it at all. It's just a thought.

506
00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:07.220
Now, there are three types of delusions.

507
00:34:07.220 --> 00:34:14.220
The first is a paranoid delusion, which is a false, fixed belief that people are out to harm you.

508
00:34:14.220 --> 00:34:18.220
Like the police are out to kill me. The mock is out to kill me.

509
00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:22.220
My wife is having me committed so she can run off with the psychiatrist.

510
00:34:22.220 --> 00:34:25.220
The kids are lying about me so they can get my house.

511
00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:30.220
The neighbors are shining lasers on me to hurt me, that kind of stuff.

512
00:34:30.220 --> 00:34:35.220
Grandiose delusion is a false, fixed belief that you are superior.

513
00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:43.220
So this is where you think you're Christ or Muhammad or Genghis Khan or whomever.

514
00:34:43.220 --> 00:34:47.220
Or you generically think that you're the world's smartest person.

515
00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:49.220
You're the world's greatest person.

516
00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:53.220
The future of the universe hinges on you.

517
00:34:53.220 --> 00:34:56.220
Those are all grandiose thoughts.

518
00:34:56.220 --> 00:35:04.220
The third type of delusion is a somatic, which is a false, fixed belief about bodies, about a body part, about your body.

519
00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:10.220
In other words, I have x-ray vision. I can melt stone with my eyes.

520
00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:16.220
My brain is a Martian superconducting proton accelerator.

521
00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:21.220
There are worms inside my arm. My body is hollow.

522
00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:25.220
You're pregnant. You're 83 and you're male.

523
00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:33.220
Those are delusions, somatic delusions.

524
00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:37.220
Now hallucinations, how are hallucinations different?

525
00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:42.220
A hallucination is a false, fixed sensory experience.

526
00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:45.220
You see with a delusion, there was no sensation going on.

527
00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:49.220
With hallucination, it's purely sensory.

528
00:35:49.220 --> 00:35:55.220
You see, hear, feel, taste, smell, all those things.

529
00:35:55.220 --> 00:35:57.220
So how many senses do we have? We have five.

530
00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:03.220
So how many types of hallucinations? Five, one, four, each sense.

531
00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:08.220
The most common hallucination is letter A, A auditory.

532
00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:15.220
An auditory, A-U-D-I-T-R-Y, auditory hallucination, where you hear things.

533
00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:21.220
Primarily, they are voices telling you to hurt yourself.

534
00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:25.220
So the most common hallucination is an auditory hallucination.

535
00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:31.220
The most common auditory hallucination is voices telling you to harm yourself.

536
00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:40.220
That is by far and away the most common hallucination reported.

537
00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:47.220
The next most common hallucination is a visual, which is seeing things that are not there.

538
00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:56.220
The third most common is tactile, T-A-C-T-I-L-E, which is feeling things that are not there.

539
00:36:56.220 --> 00:37:07.220
And the last two are gust-tatori, G-U-S, and then the word tat, T-A-T, and then O-R-Y, gust-tatori,

540
00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:12.220
which is smelling or tasting, I'm sorry, tasting things that are not there.

541
00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:24.220
And the last one is olfactory, O-L, and then the word factory, which is smelling things that are not real.

542
00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:31.220
So you have auditory, visual, tactile, gust-tatori, olfactory.

543
00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:34.220
The first three are the most common in that order.

544
00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:41.220
The most common is auditory, second most common is visual, third most common is tactile.

545
00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:53.220
The last two, gust-tatori and olfactory, are relatively rare, and you don't usually hear of them much.

546
00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:56.220
Now let's turn the page and talk about an illusion.

547
00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:58.220
What is an illusion?

548
00:37:58.220 --> 00:38:07.220
Well, an illusion is a misinterpretation of reality, a misinterpretation of reality.

549
00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:10.220
You're misinterpreting what's going on.

550
00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:17.220
It is a sensory experience, sensory.

551
00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:22.220
Well, that's just great, because now what, too, are we going to confuse?

552
00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:25.220
Because they're both sensory.

553
00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:28.220
Hallucinations are sensory, right?

554
00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:30.220
And illusions are sensory.

555
00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:33.220
Well, that's bad, because now how are you going to tell them apart?

556
00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:35.220
And that's what that next part says.

557
00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:39.220
Number two, differentiation between illusions and hallucinations.

558
00:38:39.220 --> 00:38:44.220
In other words, how do you tell the difference between a hallucination and an illusion?

559
00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:46.220
Well, here's how you tell the difference.

560
00:38:46.220 --> 00:38:50.220
With illusions, there is a reference in reality.

561
00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:55.220
With an illusion, there is a referent in reality.

562
00:38:55.220 --> 00:38:58.220
Now let's talk about what that means.

563
00:38:58.220 --> 00:39:06.220
A referent, R-E-F-E-R-E-N-T, R-E-F-E-R-E-N-T, referent.

564
00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:15.220
What a referent is, is something to which a person refers when they say something.

565
00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:19.220
So with an illusion, there is a referent.

566
00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:22.220
There's actually something there.

567
00:39:22.220 --> 00:39:25.220
They just misinterpret what it is.

568
00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:30.220
With a hallucination, there is absolutely nothing there.

569
00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:35.220
So it's probably best understood by illustration.

570
00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:43.220
If a client says, listen, I hear demon voices.

571
00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:48.220
Listen, I hear demon voices.

572
00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:51.220
Of what is that an example?

573
00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:54.220
That is a hallucination. Why?

574
00:39:54.220 --> 00:39:58.220
It was sensory and there was nothing there.

575
00:39:58.220 --> 00:40:00.220
What about this example?

576
00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:08.220
A client overhears nurses and doctors laughing and talking at the nurses station

577
00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:14.220
and says, listen, I hear demon voices.

578
00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:17.220
Now they said the exact same thing.

579
00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:19.220
Of what is that an example?

580
00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:22.220
Why is that an illusion? It was the same thing.

581
00:40:22.220 --> 00:40:25.220
They said the exact same thing.

582
00:40:25.220 --> 00:40:30.220
The question bothered to tell you that there was actually real people talking,

583
00:40:30.220 --> 00:40:34.220
making real sounds that they misinterpreted.

584
00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:38.220
In that case, it's an illusion.

585
00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:40.220
What's this?

586
00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:46.220
During your interview, a client stares at the wall and says, look, I see a bomb.

587
00:40:46.220 --> 00:40:49.220
They stare at the wall and say, look, I see a bomb.

588
00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:51.220
B-O-M-B.

589
00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:55.220
Hallucination or illusion? Hallucination.

590
00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:57.220
What about this?

591
00:40:57.220 --> 00:41:03.220
During your interview, the client looks at the fire extinguisher on the wall

592
00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:06.220
and says, look, I see a bomb.

593
00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:10.220
What's that? That's an illusion. Why?

594
00:41:10.220 --> 00:41:12.220
Was there something there? Was there a referent?

595
00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:15.220
Was there something in reality to which they referred?

596
00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:18.220
Yes, that was the fire extinguisher.

597
00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:24.220
So is it really going to be hard to spot an illusion on the test?

598
00:41:24.220 --> 00:41:27.220
Why is it going to be easy?

599
00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:29.220
Because they're going to add a whole bunch of words,

600
00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:33.220
a whole big sentence telling you there was actually something there.

601
00:41:33.220 --> 00:41:40.220
And when you see all that there, you go, oh, that's an illusion.

602
00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:48.220
So it's sort of like an illusion is a hallucination with a referent, basically.

603
00:41:48.220 --> 00:41:51.220
Now, the bigger question is letter E.

604
00:41:51.220 --> 00:41:58.220
How do you deal with these psychotic symptoms in a psychotic patient?

605
00:41:58.220 --> 00:42:04.220
When dealing with a patient experiencing psychotic symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations, and illusions,

606
00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:08.220
the first thing you, the nurse, must ask yourself is, what is their problem?

607
00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:12.220
In other words, what kind of psychosis do they have?

608
00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:19.220
I hope you guys were taught that there are basically three types of psychosis.

609
00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:23.220
Were you taught that?

610
00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:27.220
Because if you weren't, I don't understand how you're supposed to answer questions.

611
00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:31.220
Because these three different types are huge differences.

612
00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:35.220
The first type of psychosis is what they call a functional psychosis.

613
00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:38.220
Now, you don't have to know the names of all of these.

614
00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:45.220
You just have to know there are three types and what they mean.

615
00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:48.220
The first type of psychotic is a functional psychotic.

616
00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:51.220
And what do you suppose that means?

617
00:42:51.220 --> 00:42:55.220
Functional.

618
00:42:55.220 --> 00:42:57.220
They can function in every day.

619
00:42:57.220 --> 00:42:58.220
Like, can they have a family?

620
00:42:58.220 --> 00:42:59.220
A marriage?

621
00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:00.220
A relationship?

622
00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:01.220
A job?

623
00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:02.220
They can do all that.

624
00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:06.220
They can live alone, take care of themselves, pay their bills, pay their taxes.

625
00:43:06.220 --> 00:43:07.220
They can function.

626
00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:09.220
But they are what?

627
00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:10.220
Psychotic.

628
00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:12.220
They can function, but they're psychotic.

629
00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:18.220
Now, there are four diseases that make up 90% of this category.

630
00:43:18.220 --> 00:43:23.220
And they are what I call the schizoschezo major manics.

631
00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:26.220
Now, what are those?

632
00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:31.220
Schizo for schizophrenia.

633
00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:34.220
The second schizo is schizoaffective disorder.

634
00:43:34.220 --> 00:43:37.220
I hope you've heard of that.

635
00:43:37.220 --> 00:43:40.220
The third one is major depression.

636
00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:43.220
Major depression, not depression.

637
00:43:43.220 --> 00:43:44.220
You understand?

638
00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:46.220
Depression is not psychotic.

639
00:43:46.220 --> 00:43:50.220
Major depression is psychotic.

640
00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.220
So that's a major there.

641
00:43:52.220 --> 00:43:54.220
And then manic.

642
00:43:54.220 --> 00:43:58.220
People that are acutely manic.

643
00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:03.220
So are bi-polars functional?

644
00:44:03.220 --> 00:44:04.220
Yes.

645
00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:05.220
Yes.

646
00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:08.220
But are they always psychotic?

647
00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:09.220
No.

648
00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:11.220
No, they're only psychotic in what phase?

649
00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:12.220
The manic phase.

650
00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:14.220
The rest of their life, they're...

651
00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:17.220
In fact, you guys could be bi-polars and I wouldn't even know it.

652
00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:21.220
You could all be bi-polars for all I know.

653
00:44:22.220 --> 00:44:27.220
So what are the four functional psychosis?

654
00:44:27.220 --> 00:44:33.220
Schizo, major manic.

655
00:44:33.220 --> 00:44:34.220
All right.

656
00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:39.220
Now, the next psychotic is the psychosis of dementia.

657
00:44:39.220 --> 00:44:42.220
Dementia.

658
00:44:42.220 --> 00:44:44.220
So what's these people's problem?

659
00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:45.220
Why are they psychotic?

660
00:44:45.220 --> 00:44:51.220
Why do they not know reality and have no insight?

661
00:44:55.220 --> 00:45:00.220
Why do dementia clients not know reality and why are they not having insight?

662
00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:03.220
Why do they lack insight?

663
00:45:03.220 --> 00:45:04.220
What's that?

664
00:45:04.220 --> 00:45:10.220
Well, why is the memory messed up?

665
00:45:10.220 --> 00:45:12.220
But I heard it.

666
00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:15.220
Brain damage.

667
00:45:15.220 --> 00:45:19.220
Actual damage to the brain.

668
00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:22.220
The brain is actually damaged.

669
00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:26.220
Now, in the functional, is the brain damaged?

670
00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:28.220
No.

671
00:45:28.220 --> 00:45:33.220
It's just the chemicals are out of balance and they don't really have it.

672
00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:37.220
They have learned adaptive behaviors well.

673
00:45:37.220 --> 00:45:42.220
But in this case with the dementia, there's actual brain damage.

674
00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:45.220
And that's why they're psychotic, the brain damage.

675
00:45:45.220 --> 00:45:50.220
These would be people like Alzheimer's.

676
00:45:50.220 --> 00:45:56.220
Did you ever note anybody that after a stroke, they're kind of psychotic after a stroke due to the damage?

677
00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:57.220
That would be this.

678
00:45:57.220 --> 00:46:00.220
Have you heard of organic brain syndrome?

679
00:46:00.220 --> 00:46:04.220
That would fall under this category.

680
00:46:04.220 --> 00:46:08.220
Anything that says senile or dementia falls in this category.

681
00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:22.220
And lastly, number three, the third type of psychotic is psychotic delirium.

682
00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:23.220
Delirium.

683
00:46:23.220 --> 00:46:26.220
D-E-L-I-R-I-U-M.

684
00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:29.220
D-E-L-I-R-I-U-M delirium.

685
00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:34.220
And we'll talk about these people.

686
00:46:34.220 --> 00:46:36.220
But here's the deal.

687
00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:42.220
What's the first thing you must do to get a psych question correct?

688
00:46:42.220 --> 00:46:46.220
Psychotic or non-psychotic?

689
00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:47.220
Psychotic or non-psychotic?

690
00:46:47.220 --> 00:46:52.220
If you decide they're non-psychotic, what answers do you pick?

691
00:46:52.220 --> 00:46:57.220
The best good therapeutic communication response.

692
00:46:58.220 --> 00:47:00.220
It would be true for anybody.

693
00:47:00.220 --> 00:47:05.220
If you decide that they are psychotic, then what step must you take?

694
00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:07.220
Functional or non-psychotic?

695
00:47:07.220 --> 00:47:12.220
Decide which of the three categories that person falls in.

696
00:47:12.220 --> 00:47:13.220
Are they functional?

697
00:47:13.220 --> 00:47:15.220
Are they dementia?

698
00:47:15.220 --> 00:47:17.220
Are they delirious?

699
00:47:17.220 --> 00:47:21.220
Because you're going to use different approaches for each of these.

700
00:47:21.220 --> 00:47:25.220
Because not all psychotics are the same.

701
00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:29.220
So let me illustrate how you're going to answer questions differently.

702
00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:34.220
Letter F, let's talk about the functional psychotic.

703
00:47:34.220 --> 00:47:36.220
Does this person have brain damage?

704
00:47:36.220 --> 00:47:38.220
No.

705
00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:45.220
So number one, this patient has the potential to learn reality.

706
00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:49.220
Why can they learn reality?

707
00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:50.220
They don't have any damage.

708
00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:52.220
They can learn it.

709
00:47:52.220 --> 00:47:55.220
They might need some meds to balance some chemicals,

710
00:47:55.220 --> 00:47:59.220
and then you might need to set some structure, but they can learn it.

711
00:47:59.220 --> 00:48:03.220
They can improve.

712
00:48:03.220 --> 00:48:07.220
Well then how, if they can learn it, what's your role as a nurse?

713
00:48:07.220 --> 00:48:11.220
If they can learn reality, what is your role as a nurse?

714
00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:13.220
Teach reality.

715
00:48:13.220 --> 00:48:15.220
So how do you teach reality?

716
00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:18.220
You use the four-step process that's here.

717
00:48:18.220 --> 00:48:26.220
This four-step process is the way we teach reality to a functional.

718
00:48:26.220 --> 00:48:32.220
First step, acknowledge feeling.

719
00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:38.220
Second step, present reality.

720
00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:42.220
Third step, set a limit.

721
00:48:42.220 --> 00:48:49.220
Second step, enforce the limit.

722
00:48:49.220 --> 00:48:53.220
Now, that isn't good enough for me just to tell you that.

723
00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:59.220
I need to illustrate to you how that would look in a question.

724
00:48:59.220 --> 00:49:02.220
Because boards doesn't say, what's the first thing you do?

725
00:49:02.220 --> 00:49:04.220
A, acknowledge feeling.

726
00:49:04.220 --> 00:49:05.220
They don't say that.

727
00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:07.220
They say, what's the first thing you'll say?

728
00:49:07.220 --> 00:49:09.220
And they'll give you four quotes,

729
00:49:09.220 --> 00:49:13.220
and you're supposed to pick out the one that acknowledges the feeling,

730
00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:16.220
or the one that presents reality, or the one that sets the limit.

731
00:49:16.220 --> 00:49:23.220
So what do answers look like that acknowledge feeling?

732
00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:26.220
If you said, oh, I've got to look for an answer that acknowledges their feeling,

733
00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:27.220
what would it look like?

734
00:49:27.220 --> 00:49:29.220
How would you spot it?

735
00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:32.220
What would it say?

736
00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:34.220
What are you saying to feel this and this?

737
00:49:34.220 --> 00:49:38.220
Usually the word feel is in the answer, yes.

738
00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:43.220
So you'd say, or you could even specify a feeling, couldn't you?

739
00:49:43.220 --> 00:49:48.220
If you don't say the word feel, usually there's a specification of a feeling.

740
00:49:48.220 --> 00:49:55.220
For example, you'd say, I see you're angry, or you seem upset,

741
00:49:55.220 --> 00:50:00.220
or that must be distressing, or that's so sad,

742
00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:04.220
or all that has happened has been so devastating,

743
00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:07.220
or tell me more about how you're feeling right now.

744
00:50:07.220 --> 00:50:10.220
See, all of those go to what? Feeling.

745
00:50:10.220 --> 00:50:13.220
And that's the first thing you do.

746
00:50:13.220 --> 00:50:19.220
How many have noticed that in sight, if you're between a couple of answers

747
00:50:19.220 --> 00:50:22.220
and one of them is talking about feeling,

748
00:50:22.220 --> 00:50:26.220
if you pick it, you're usually more right than wrong.

749
00:50:26.220 --> 00:50:27.220
Have you noticed that?

750
00:50:27.220 --> 00:50:28.220
Because what are you doing?

751
00:50:28.220 --> 00:50:31.220
Acknowledging their feeling,

752
00:50:31.220 --> 00:50:36.220
which is the very first thing you should always do with a functional.

753
00:50:36.220 --> 00:50:38.220
Secondly, you present reality.

754
00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:40.220
Well, how does presenting reality look like?

755
00:50:40.220 --> 00:50:42.220
Well, it has different forms.

756
00:50:42.220 --> 00:50:46.220
One of the classic forms of presenting reality says,

757
00:50:46.220 --> 00:50:53.220
I know that blank is real to you, but I do not blank.

758
00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:54.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

759
00:50:54.220 --> 00:51:01.220
I know that you see that demon, but I do not see a demon.

760
00:51:01.220 --> 00:51:02.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

761
00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:05.220
I know you, I understand those voices are real to you,

762
00:51:05.220 --> 00:51:09.220
but I do not hear any voices.

763
00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:10.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

764
00:51:10.220 --> 00:51:13.220
I understand that you think that the mafia is out to kill you,

765
00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:16.220
but in reality, no one is going to harm you.

766
00:51:16.220 --> 00:51:19.220
Do you see that? That's one format.

767
00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:24.220
The other format of presenting reality is just to tell them what is real.

768
00:51:24.220 --> 00:51:29.220
I am a nurse, this is a hospital, and this is your breakfast.

769
00:51:29.220 --> 00:51:30.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

770
00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:34.220
So you can either tell them what actually reality is,

771
00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:37.220
or you can just say, I know that's real to you, but I do not.

772
00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:41.220
Both of those are acceptable formats for presenting reality.

773
00:51:41.220 --> 00:51:45.220
Either one is good, but it's not the first thing you do,

774
00:51:45.220 --> 00:51:48.220
it's the second thing you do.

775
00:51:48.220 --> 00:51:54.220
Setting a limit, what setting a limit looks like is when you set a limit,

776
00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:56.220
the answer will say something like,

777
00:51:56.220 --> 00:52:01.220
that topic is off limits in our conversation.

778
00:52:01.220 --> 00:52:06.220
Or when we talk together, we are not going to address that problem,

779
00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:09.220
or we're not going to talk about that.

780
00:52:09.220 --> 00:52:14.220
And sometimes you can go all the way and be as extreme as,

781
00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:16.220
stop talking about.

782
00:52:16.220 --> 00:52:24.220
That would be all right to tell them to stop talking about those aliens.

783
00:52:24.220 --> 00:52:28.220
Stop talking about the voices.

784
00:52:28.220 --> 00:52:31.220
And you can be that strong, or you can be the strong enough to say,

785
00:52:31.220 --> 00:52:36.220
we're not going to talk about those voices.

786
00:52:36.220 --> 00:52:39.220
You see, you can be that directive and that strong.

787
00:52:39.220 --> 00:52:46.220
And then enforcing a limit usually takes the format of saying something like,

788
00:52:46.220 --> 00:52:52.220
I see you're too ill to stay reality based, so our conversation is over.

789
00:52:52.220 --> 00:52:55.220
That's enforcing the limit.

790
00:52:55.220 --> 00:52:58.220
Enforcing the limit is ending the conversation.

791
00:52:58.220 --> 00:53:03.220
Enforcing the limit is not taking away a privilege.

792
00:53:03.220 --> 00:53:05.220
You see, that's punishing.

793
00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:10.220
Does everybody understand the difference between enforcing a limit and punishing them?

794
00:53:10.220 --> 00:53:12.220
What are you doing when you punish somebody?

795
00:53:12.220 --> 00:53:16.220
What actions do you take to punish?

796
00:53:16.220 --> 00:53:23.220
What would be bad answers that would be construed as punishing?

797
00:53:23.220 --> 00:53:26.220
Because you've told them to stop talking about the voices, right?

798
00:53:26.220 --> 00:53:29.220
And they keep talking about the voices. They violate the limit.

799
00:53:29.220 --> 00:53:33.220
So what would be a bad way to enforce it?

800
00:53:33.220 --> 00:53:36.220
Such as?

801
00:53:36.220 --> 00:53:42.220
Yeah, you can note, since you can't follow the rules, you lose your telephone privileges.

802
00:53:42.220 --> 00:53:45.220
You lose your refrigerator privileges.

803
00:53:45.220 --> 00:53:49.220
You're going to be restricted to your room.

804
00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:52.220
No, that's not enforcing the limit.

805
00:53:52.220 --> 00:53:54.220
That's punishing them.

806
00:53:54.220 --> 00:53:59.220
The only enforcement is ending the conversation.

807
00:53:59.220 --> 00:54:03.220
That's the enforcement.

808
00:54:03.220 --> 00:54:06.220
Why is that appropriate?

809
00:54:06.220 --> 00:54:13.220
Why is it good to end a conversation with them when they are not reality based

810
00:54:13.220 --> 00:54:17.220
and won't stay reality based?

811
00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:21.220
You're not going to accomplish anything, and by continuing to talk, what are you reinforcing?

812
00:54:21.220 --> 00:54:24.220
The non-reality.

813
00:54:24.220 --> 00:54:26.220
So how does it go?

814
00:54:26.220 --> 00:54:28.220
Well, let's talk about this.

815
00:54:28.220 --> 00:54:31.220
You have a patient who says to you, and he schizophrenics.

816
00:54:31.220 --> 00:54:33.220
Does that matter?

817
00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:36.220
Why does that matter?

818
00:54:36.220 --> 00:54:39.220
It throws him in what category?

819
00:54:39.220 --> 00:54:40.220
Functional.

820
00:54:40.220 --> 00:54:43.220
And you know to do the four-step functional process.

821
00:54:43.220 --> 00:54:47.220
He says, schizophrenics says to you, I'm going to kill you all.

822
00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:49.220
You're all going to be dead by morning.

823
00:54:49.220 --> 00:54:53.220
I'm going to slit your throats, and I'm starting with you.

824
00:54:53.220 --> 00:54:55.220
Okay?

825
00:54:55.220 --> 00:55:00.220
Rather paranoid, violent, delusional thinking.

826
00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:03.220
What's the first thing you say to him?

827
00:55:03.220 --> 00:55:04.220
I understand your upset.

828
00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:06.220
What's that?

829
00:55:06.220 --> 00:55:07.220
I understand your upset.

830
00:55:07.220 --> 00:55:08.220
Right.

831
00:55:08.220 --> 00:55:12.220
You can say that I understand your upset, or you can say, I see you're angry.

832
00:55:12.220 --> 00:55:14.220
I wouldn't say, how do you feel right now?

833
00:55:14.220 --> 00:55:16.220
Because I think I know.

834
00:55:16.220 --> 00:55:22.220
You know, I mean, sometimes they give you enough data in the question to kind of know what they're feeling like.

835
00:55:22.220 --> 00:55:25.220
Okay, so I see you are upset.

836
00:55:25.220 --> 00:55:28.220
Ben, what do you do?

837
00:55:28.220 --> 00:55:31.220
What did I tell you to do next?

838
00:55:31.220 --> 00:55:33.220
Okay, but they're not going to say present reality.

839
00:55:33.220 --> 00:55:34.220
Give me a quote.

840
00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:37.220
In this case, you'd say, I see you are upset.

841
00:55:37.220 --> 00:55:39.220
But there are no bows in the wall.

842
00:55:39.220 --> 00:55:41.220
But he didn't talk about bugs.

843
00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:42.220
That's the only problem.

844
00:55:42.220 --> 00:55:44.220
That is presenting reality.

845
00:55:44.220 --> 00:55:45.220
But he didn't say that.

846
00:55:45.220 --> 00:55:47.220
He said he was going to kill us all.

847
00:55:47.220 --> 00:55:49.220
He said we're all going to be dead by morning.

848
00:55:49.220 --> 00:55:52.220
He's going to slit our throats, and he's going to start with me.

849
00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:56.220
Killing us is an acceptable behavior.

850
00:55:56.220 --> 00:56:00.220
Okay, and I think that's a great answer.

851
00:56:00.220 --> 00:56:06.220
I think we could go better with talking about it more positively than negatively.

852
00:56:06.220 --> 00:56:13.220
Rather than saying, you're not going to kill anybody, I would say, we're all going to be kept safe.

853
00:56:13.220 --> 00:56:14.220
I would present the reality.

854
00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:26.220
And always try to, if you're between two things, sometimes, Derek, it comes down to one is stated positively and one is stated negatively, and you always want to...

855
00:56:26.220 --> 00:56:36.220
Rather than saying, you're on a fluid, you're only allowed to have 10 cc's at breakfast time.

856
00:56:36.220 --> 00:56:39.220
You say, you can have 10 cc's at breakfast time.

857
00:56:39.220 --> 00:56:40.220
You know what I mean?

858
00:56:40.220 --> 00:56:42.220
You always like it as stated positively.

859
00:56:42.220 --> 00:56:48.220
Like with diets, it's better to emphasize what they can have than what they cannot have.

860
00:56:48.220 --> 00:56:52.220
With mobility, it's better to tell them what they can do.

861
00:56:52.220 --> 00:56:58.220
And so in every way, if you're between two answers, one is positive, one is negative, go with a positive.

862
00:56:58.220 --> 00:56:59.220
Does that make sense?

863
00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:04.220
Okay, in this case, Derek said what...

864
00:57:04.220 --> 00:57:06.220
Killing us is an...

865
00:57:06.220 --> 00:57:10.220
Killing is inappropriate and not going to happen in this situation.

866
00:57:10.220 --> 00:57:19.220
Versus, I see you are upset, but everybody is going to be kept safe while we're here.

867
00:57:19.220 --> 00:57:20.220
This is the same...

868
00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:23.220
You see, it's the same thing, only stated more positively.

869
00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:25.220
It's presenting the reality of what's going to happen.

870
00:57:25.220 --> 00:57:28.220
No one is going to get killed, right?

871
00:57:28.220 --> 00:57:33.220
Okay, then what do you say?

872
00:57:33.220 --> 00:57:35.220
We're not going to talk about that kind of stuff.

873
00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:41.220
We're just... those kinds of ideas, although they are real to you, are off limits in our conversation.

874
00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:43.220
So then he keeps talking about it.

875
00:57:43.220 --> 00:57:44.220
I'm going to kill you.

876
00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:45.220
I'm going to slit your throat.

877
00:57:45.220 --> 00:57:47.220
I've got this knife.

878
00:57:47.220 --> 00:57:48.220
I'm going to shank you.

879
00:57:48.220 --> 00:57:51.220
What do they say?

880
00:57:51.220 --> 00:57:54.220
What do you then say?

881
00:57:54.220 --> 00:57:56.220
I see you're too ill.

882
00:57:56.220 --> 00:58:00.220
It's a reality-based conversation, so our conversation is over.

883
00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:04.220
And in reality, in real life, when I work psych, I net off with them a tranquilizer.

884
00:58:04.220 --> 00:58:08.220
I say, and we have some medication that can help you control those things.

885
00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:10.220
Would you like some?

886
00:58:10.220 --> 00:58:12.220
You know, and that kind of thing.

887
00:58:12.220 --> 00:58:14.220
A lot of times they do.

888
00:58:14.220 --> 00:58:18.220
And I always like to say that because I like to say, I see you're too ill.

889
00:58:18.220 --> 00:58:24.220
I love to say that because what's it telling them about their experience, that that is a symptom of their illness?

890
00:58:24.220 --> 00:58:25.220
And they're gaining...

891
00:58:25.220 --> 00:58:28.220
I'm hoping to teach them that it's not them.

892
00:58:28.220 --> 00:58:33.220
It's their illness, and they need to recognize what's ill, me, and what's healthy, me.

893
00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:39.220
And so I always love to say, I know that's a symptom of your illness, but...

894
00:58:39.220 --> 00:58:43.220
No, I see you're too ill to have a reality-based conversation.

895
00:58:43.220 --> 00:58:45.220
So we're done now.

896
00:58:45.220 --> 00:58:49.220
But I always like to say, but we have medication to help you with those symptoms.

897
00:58:49.220 --> 00:58:52.220
Now, again, what am I reinforcing?

898
00:58:52.220 --> 00:58:59.220
That this is a symptom of your illness, and it's controllable, and we have meds for it, so comply with it.

899
00:58:59.220 --> 00:59:00.220
You know what I mean?

900
00:59:00.220 --> 00:59:05.220
Because noncompliance is the number one reason why psych patients are readmitted to facilities, correct?

901
00:59:05.220 --> 00:59:07.220
So I always find that, and then I say...

902
00:59:07.220 --> 00:59:08.220
And they say, well, what medication?

903
00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:09.220
Well, like Xanax, or what?

904
00:59:09.220 --> 00:59:10.220
Well, I don't like, okay, well, you have this.

905
00:59:10.220 --> 00:59:12.220
Okay, well, I'll have some of that, I guess.

906
00:59:12.220 --> 00:59:14.220
It works at about one-third of the time.

907
00:59:14.220 --> 00:59:16.220
You say one-third of the time is a very good...

908
00:59:16.220 --> 00:59:17.220
Oh, it's a lot.

909
00:59:17.220 --> 00:59:19.220
I mean, that's pretty good for a psych hospital.

910
00:59:19.220 --> 00:59:21.220
One-third compliance rate.

911
00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:24.220
So then they do well.

912
00:59:24.220 --> 00:59:25.220
Okay?

913
00:59:25.220 --> 00:59:26.220
So that's a good way to go.

914
00:59:26.220 --> 00:59:27.220
You get the...

915
00:59:27.220 --> 00:59:29.220
Do you understand the process?

916
00:59:29.220 --> 00:59:33.220
A knowledge feeling, present reality, set a limit, enforce.

917
00:59:33.220 --> 00:59:36.220
However, if they have psychosis of dementia...

918
00:59:36.220 --> 00:59:37.220
Who are these people?

919
00:59:37.220 --> 00:59:39.220
Dementias.

920
00:59:39.220 --> 00:59:46.220
Pheniles, Alzheimer's, brain injuries, organic brain syndrome, post-strokes.

921
00:59:46.220 --> 00:59:55.220
Number one says this patient has a brain problem, structural brain problem, and cannot learn reality.

922
00:59:55.220 --> 00:59:59.220
They cannot learn reality.

923
00:59:59.220 --> 01:00:02.220
Do you think that makes any difference?

924
01:00:02.220 --> 01:00:04.220
What about the functional?

925
01:00:04.220 --> 01:00:05.220
What's the high...

926
01:00:05.220 --> 01:00:08.220
What's the positive point about functional?

927
01:00:08.220 --> 01:00:10.220
They can learn reality.

928
01:00:10.220 --> 01:00:12.220
What's the best for a dementia?

929
01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:13.220
They can't learn it.

930
01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:17.220
Do you think that difference is minor or major?

931
01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:18.220
That's a major difference.

932
01:00:18.220 --> 01:00:23.220
Do you see why you have to differentiate which of the three categories your psychotic falls into?

933
01:00:24.220 --> 01:00:27.220
Because it makes a huge difference in how you deal with it.

934
01:00:27.220 --> 01:00:29.220
Well, how do you deal with a dementia?

935
01:00:29.220 --> 01:00:34.220
A psychotic dementia, a person who has brain damage, and they can't learn reality.

936
01:00:34.220 --> 01:00:37.220
Well, number one, you do two steps.

937
01:00:37.220 --> 01:00:44.220
The first step is you acknowledge their feeling, and the second step is you redirect them.

938
01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:46.220
You redirect them.

939
01:00:46.220 --> 01:00:49.220
So what am I telling you you don't do?

940
01:00:49.220 --> 01:00:51.220
Present reality.

941
01:00:51.220 --> 01:00:54.220
Present reality.

942
01:00:54.220 --> 01:01:00.220
Why is presenting reality for a dementia not appropriate?

943
01:01:00.220 --> 01:01:02.220
They can't learn it.

944
01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:05.220
And you keep trying to teach them that they can't learn.

945
01:01:05.220 --> 01:01:07.220
It's ridiculous.

946
01:01:07.220 --> 01:01:13.220
It's going to frustrate them, anger them, discourage you.

947
01:01:13.220 --> 01:01:15.220
You can't.

948
01:01:15.220 --> 01:01:17.220
They can't learn it.

949
01:01:17.220 --> 01:01:18.220
It's impossible.

950
01:01:18.220 --> 01:01:20.220
So don't try.

951
01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:22.220
So what do you do?

952
01:01:22.220 --> 01:01:30.220
You redirect them, and redirect means to channel them from something they cannot do to something that they can do.

953
01:01:34.220 --> 01:01:39.220
Why do you not set limits on their bizarre communication?

954
01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:43.220
It's just plain mean, isn't it?

955
01:01:43.220 --> 01:01:44.220
That's just mean.

956
01:01:44.220 --> 01:01:46.220
Now, I do want to caution you guys.

957
01:01:46.220 --> 01:01:53.220
Here's where a lot of people go wrong, and they get confused, and there shouldn't be any cause for confusion, if you understand this.

958
01:01:53.220 --> 01:02:01.220
With your dementias, like your Alzheimer's and your C-Nas, when you work with them on the unit, what do they tend to get all the time?

959
01:02:01.220 --> 01:02:04.220
What's a problem they always have?

960
01:02:04.220 --> 01:02:08.220
You ever worked with elderly dementias?

961
01:02:08.220 --> 01:02:13.220
What's a problem they constantly have every day related to because they forget?

962
01:02:13.220 --> 01:02:17.220
Where they are, where their room is, what day it is.

963
01:02:17.220 --> 01:02:18.220
Okay?

964
01:02:18.220 --> 01:02:21.220
Now, that is not psychosis.

965
01:02:21.220 --> 01:02:23.220
At that point, they are not psychotic.

966
01:02:23.220 --> 01:02:25.220
They're just forgetting.

967
01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:30.220
Now, when they're having delusions, hallucinations, and illusions, what are they at that point?

968
01:02:30.220 --> 01:02:31.220
Psychotic.

969
01:02:31.220 --> 01:02:33.220
Okay, now, here's the thing.

970
01:02:33.220 --> 01:02:41.220
I told you what technique is not appropriate for a dementia client when they're experiencing psychotic symptoms.

971
01:02:41.220 --> 01:02:44.220
What is not appropriate for presenting reality?

972
01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:52.220
Don't confuse that with reality orientation.

973
01:02:52.220 --> 01:02:55.220
What is reality orientation?

974
01:02:55.220 --> 01:03:01.220
What does that mean when you see the phrase reality orientation?

975
01:03:01.220 --> 01:03:05.220
Tell them person, place, and time.

976
01:03:05.220 --> 01:03:08.220
Is that appropriate with dementias?

977
01:03:08.220 --> 01:03:09.220
Yes.

978
01:03:09.220 --> 01:03:14.220
Reality orientation is always appropriate with dementias.

979
01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:19.220
Is presenting reality appropriate with dementias?

980
01:03:19.220 --> 01:03:20.220
No.

981
01:03:20.220 --> 01:03:24.220
Do you see the problem with our language that confuses people?

982
01:03:24.220 --> 01:03:27.220
Presenting reality versus reality orientation.

983
01:03:27.220 --> 01:03:30.220
Two totally different things.

984
01:03:30.220 --> 01:03:33.220
Can you do reality orientation with a dementia?

985
01:03:33.220 --> 01:03:34.220
Yes.

986
01:03:34.220 --> 01:03:37.220
Yes, because you're just dealing with loss of memory.

987
01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:43.220
Can you do presentation of reality to dementia patients?

988
01:03:43.220 --> 01:03:44.220
No.

989
01:03:44.220 --> 01:03:45.220
No.

990
01:03:45.220 --> 01:03:46.220
Can you keep that straight?

991
01:03:46.220 --> 01:03:52.220
Because a lot of people are going to confuse that, and I don't want you to confuse it.

992
01:03:52.220 --> 01:03:55.220
Let me illustrate my point here.

993
01:03:55.220 --> 01:03:57.220
You have a patient with Alzheimer's.

994
01:03:57.220 --> 01:03:59.220
Did that matter?

995
01:03:59.220 --> 01:04:01.220
In the question, did that phrase matter?

996
01:04:01.220 --> 01:04:02.220
Why?

997
01:04:02.220 --> 01:04:05.220
It threw him in the dementia category.

998
01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:12.220
We knew they were psychotic now, and it threw him in the dementia category.

999
01:04:12.220 --> 01:04:14.220
So you've got a patient with Alzheimer's.

1000
01:04:14.220 --> 01:04:18.220
She's in the waiting room, the lobby of the nursing home.

1001
01:04:18.220 --> 01:04:19.220
She's all dressed up.

1002
01:04:19.220 --> 01:04:21.220
It's Sunday.

1003
01:04:21.220 --> 01:04:25.220
You say to her, Mrs. Smith, you're all dressed up.

1004
01:04:25.220 --> 01:04:27.220
She says, yes, my husband's picking me up.

1005
01:04:27.220 --> 01:04:30.220
We're going to go to church.

1006
01:04:30.220 --> 01:04:34.220
Okay.

1007
01:04:34.220 --> 01:04:41.220
Problem, husband has been dead for 10 years.

1008
01:04:41.220 --> 01:04:44.220
So she has a what?

1009
01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:48.220
She thinks her husband is alive, but he's been dead.

1010
01:04:48.220 --> 01:04:50.220
So she has a what?

1011
01:04:50.220 --> 01:04:52.220
True or false?

1012
01:04:52.220 --> 01:04:55.220
False.

1013
01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:56.220
Fixed.

1014
01:04:56.220 --> 01:04:57.220
Bullied.

1015
01:04:57.220 --> 01:04:59.220
So she has a what?

1016
01:04:59.220 --> 01:05:00.220
Delusion.

1017
01:05:00.220 --> 01:05:01.220
So she is delusional.

1018
01:05:01.220 --> 01:05:03.220
So at this point, she is what?

1019
01:05:03.220 --> 01:05:05.220
A

1020
01:05:05.220 --> 01:05:08.220
What do you say to her?

1021
01:05:08.220 --> 01:05:12.220
So what would be the first thing you'd say to her?

1022
01:05:12.220 --> 01:05:13.220
My husband's coming to pick me up.

1023
01:05:13.220 --> 01:05:17.220
We're going to church.

1024
01:05:17.220 --> 01:05:20.220
What does it say to do?

1025
01:05:20.220 --> 01:05:22.220
Okay.

1026
01:05:22.220 --> 01:05:24.220
Says to acknowledge feeling.

1027
01:05:24.220 --> 01:05:25.220
Okay.

1028
01:05:25.220 --> 01:05:27.220
In this case, what would that look like?

1029
01:05:27.220 --> 01:05:30.220
What's that?

1030
01:05:30.220 --> 01:05:35.220
There's no feeling there, though.

1031
01:05:35.220 --> 01:05:37.220
No, I wouldn't say are you.

1032
01:05:37.220 --> 01:05:41.220
I would say that sounds exciting.

1033
01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:44.220
Or that sounds like an exciting thing to do.

1034
01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:46.220
Or that sounds fun.

1035
01:05:46.220 --> 01:05:50.220
Or that sounds interesting.

1036
01:05:50.220 --> 01:05:52.220
Don't always ask.

1037
01:05:52.220 --> 01:05:59.220
See, a lot of times when these questions, they tell you stuff so that you are supposed to recognize the feeling.

1038
01:05:59.220 --> 01:06:03.220
So don't always ask what are you, are you.

1039
01:06:03.220 --> 01:06:05.220
Just say I see you are happy.

1040
01:06:05.220 --> 01:06:06.220
I see you are sad.

1041
01:06:06.220 --> 01:06:09.220
You know, if it fits, those are much better answers.

1042
01:06:09.220 --> 01:06:11.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

1043
01:06:11.220 --> 01:06:13.220
Because you can say how are you feeling?

1044
01:06:13.220 --> 01:06:14.220
You can say what I think I'm feeling.

1045
01:06:14.220 --> 01:06:15.220
You know what I mean?

1046
01:06:15.220 --> 01:06:17.220
But if you say I see you're acknowledging it.

1047
01:06:17.220 --> 01:06:18.220
Do you understand what it's not?

1048
01:06:18.220 --> 01:06:22.220
What's the difference between exploring feeling and acknowledging feeling?

1049
01:06:22.220 --> 01:06:23.220
What is the difference?

1050
01:06:23.220 --> 01:06:25.220
Or is there no difference?

1051
01:06:25.220 --> 01:06:26.220
There's a difference.

1052
01:06:26.220 --> 01:06:27.220
Okay.

1053
01:06:27.220 --> 01:06:28.220
What does explore feeling mean?

1054
01:06:28.220 --> 01:06:31.220
Digging, trying to find out what it is.

1055
01:06:31.220 --> 01:06:38.220
Acknowledge means you already know you are just acknowledging with them that you see that that's what they are.

1056
01:06:38.220 --> 01:06:40.220
And that's what we are talking about here.

1057
01:06:40.220 --> 01:06:41.220
Got it?

1058
01:06:41.220 --> 01:06:42.220
Okay.

1059
01:06:42.220 --> 01:06:43.220
Then what would you say?

1060
01:06:43.220 --> 01:06:45.220
She says she's gone to church, husband driving.

1061
01:06:45.220 --> 01:06:49.220
That sounds like an exciting thing to do.

1062
01:06:49.220 --> 01:06:54.220
Yeah.

1063
01:06:54.220 --> 01:06:56.220
And she said why don't we go have some breakfast while we are waiting.

1064
01:06:56.220 --> 01:06:58.220
Now that's redirection.

1065
01:06:58.220 --> 01:07:01.220
But I don't know if it's the best one because she would say,

1066
01:07:01.220 --> 01:07:06.220
but if I go to breakfast I'll miss him, he won't, he'll miss me.

1067
01:07:06.220 --> 01:07:08.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

1068
01:07:08.220 --> 01:07:12.220
And so I think it's good that I think that you could probably say something like,

1069
01:07:12.220 --> 01:07:22.220
well why don't we sit down here and talk about what's going to happen at church today.

1070
01:07:22.220 --> 01:07:23.220
Do you see what I'm saying?

1071
01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:24.220
So while we wait.

1072
01:07:25.220 --> 01:07:29.220
So she sits down and then you start saying, well what church do you go to?

1073
01:07:29.220 --> 01:07:32.220
Who's your pastor?

1074
01:07:32.220 --> 01:07:34.220
Who's your priest?

1075
01:07:34.220 --> 01:07:36.220
What do you like about the service?

1076
01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:38.220
Do you have friends there?

1077
01:07:38.220 --> 01:07:40.220
Does your family go there?

1078
01:07:40.220 --> 01:07:42.220
What are you doing now?

1079
01:07:42.220 --> 01:07:43.220
That's all what?

1080
01:07:43.220 --> 01:07:46.220
Reinforcement of intact memory.

1081
01:07:46.220 --> 01:07:48.220
And isn't that good with dementia?

1082
01:07:48.220 --> 01:07:53.220
And pretty soon you start getting your turn from church to family.

1083
01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:56.220
See and then you say, do you have any grandkids?

1084
01:07:56.220 --> 01:08:01.220
And then you get talking about the grandkids and then you say, do you have any pictures?

1085
01:08:01.220 --> 01:08:02.220
Right?

1086
01:08:02.220 --> 01:08:06.220
And they say, yeah, could I see the pictures?

1087
01:08:06.220 --> 01:08:07.220
Well I don't have them with me.

1088
01:08:07.220 --> 01:08:11.220
Well where are they in my room?

1089
01:08:11.220 --> 01:08:12.220
Could you show me?

1090
01:08:12.220 --> 01:08:18.220
Okay, now she's leading you back to the room to show the pictures.

1091
01:08:18.220 --> 01:08:23.220
She's long forgotten about church and husband and you're not having a fight.

1092
01:08:23.220 --> 01:08:24.220
Do you see how you do it?

1093
01:08:24.220 --> 01:08:28.220
You kind of just redirect them into things.

1094
01:08:28.220 --> 01:08:30.220
So that's the way you go.

1095
01:08:30.220 --> 01:08:33.220
What would be the wrong answer to say to her?

1096
01:08:33.220 --> 01:08:36.220
That sounds exciting.

1097
01:08:36.220 --> 01:08:38.220
But your husband's been dead.

1098
01:08:38.220 --> 01:08:42.220
That's called presenting reality and that's appropriate for us.

1099
01:08:42.220 --> 01:08:44.220
Schizo, schizo, major, manic.

1100
01:08:44.220 --> 01:08:46.220
But it stinks for a dementia.

1101
01:08:46.220 --> 01:08:50.220
Do you see where one answer, the best answer for one is the worst answer for the other.

1102
01:08:50.220 --> 01:08:55.220
That's why you got to know which one you're dealing with or you're not going to know what to answer.

1103
01:08:55.220 --> 01:08:56.220
Are you seeing this?

1104
01:08:56.220 --> 01:09:05.220
Okay, let's talk about the third kind of psychotic and that's the psychotic delirium.

1105
01:09:05.220 --> 01:09:10.220
Description of this, psychotic delirium.

1106
01:09:10.220 --> 01:09:28.220
This is a temporary, sudden, dramatic, secondary loss of reality.

1107
01:09:28.220 --> 01:09:34.220
And it's usually due to some chemical imbalance in the body.

1108
01:09:34.220 --> 01:09:39.220
So it's temporary, it's sudden, it's dramatic, it's episodic.

1109
01:09:39.220 --> 01:09:48.220
It's secondary and it's usually due to some chemical imbalance in the body.

1110
01:09:48.220 --> 01:09:53.220
Now how is that different than functional?

1111
01:09:53.220 --> 01:09:54.220
It's temporary.

1112
01:09:54.220 --> 01:09:55.220
It's temporary.

1113
01:09:55.220 --> 01:09:58.220
What else is different about than this than functional?

1114
01:09:58.220 --> 01:10:00.220
It's sudden.

1115
01:10:00.220 --> 01:10:07.220
How is this different than dementia?

1116
01:10:07.220 --> 01:10:08.220
It's temporary.

1117
01:10:08.220 --> 01:10:09.220
It's sudden.

1118
01:10:09.220 --> 01:10:11.220
It's secondary.

1119
01:10:11.220 --> 01:10:13.220
You see?

1120
01:10:13.220 --> 01:10:15.220
So it's different.

1121
01:10:15.220 --> 01:10:17.220
Now who are these people?

1122
01:10:17.220 --> 01:10:27.220
These would be people that are crazy for the short term because of something else causing them to be crazy.

1123
01:10:27.220 --> 01:10:33.220
For example, have you ever seen anybody go crazy or psychotic because they're on a particular drug?

1124
01:10:34.220 --> 01:10:37.220
They have a drug reaction and it makes them lose touch with reality.

1125
01:10:37.220 --> 01:10:39.220
Have you ever seen that?

1126
01:10:39.220 --> 01:10:45.220
Tagamet will do it as innocuous as Tagamet could do it.

1127
01:10:45.220 --> 01:10:52.220
Another area is people that are high on uppers, you know, intoxicated on uppers, they'll be this way.

1128
01:10:52.220 --> 01:10:56.220
Or withdrawing from downers will be this way.

1129
01:10:56.220 --> 01:11:00.220
So delirium trevins would be this.

1130
01:11:00.220 --> 01:11:04.220
Cocaine overdose.

1131
01:11:04.220 --> 01:11:09.220
Methamphetamine overdose.

1132
01:11:09.220 --> 01:11:13.220
Have you ever, what are some other chemical situations where you've seen people act crazy?

1133
01:11:13.220 --> 01:11:14.220
It's temporary.

1134
01:11:14.220 --> 01:11:18.220
It's due to some other imbalance in their body.

1135
01:11:18.220 --> 01:11:19.220
What's that?

1136
01:11:19.220 --> 01:11:21.220
Post-op psychosis.

1137
01:11:21.220 --> 01:11:25.220
It's due to withdrawing from a downer because what's an anesthetic agent?

1138
01:11:25.220 --> 01:11:26.220
A downer.

1139
01:11:26.220 --> 01:11:27.220
What are the pain meds?

1140
01:11:27.220 --> 01:11:28.220
Downers.

1141
01:11:28.220 --> 01:11:33.220
When you withdraw from the downers, everything goes up and you have this, particularly in the elderly.

1142
01:11:33.220 --> 01:11:37.220
If you have an elderly patient who's had anesthesia and a PCA pump,

1143
01:11:37.220 --> 01:11:46.220
the third post-op day into that fourth post-op day, they can go wacky crazy for about 48 hours just playing on it.

1144
01:11:46.220 --> 01:11:48.220
And then they'll come right out of it.

1145
01:11:48.220 --> 01:11:52.220
But they will be loony as a tune.

1146
01:11:52.220 --> 01:11:57.220
So don't start zapping them full of tranquilizers or you'll confuse them,

1147
01:11:57.220 --> 01:12:04.220
and they'll open a nursing home when they would have come out of it in 48 hours and gone home.

1148
01:12:04.220 --> 01:12:05.220
Who else?

1149
01:12:05.220 --> 01:12:06.220
Post-op's classic.

1150
01:12:06.220 --> 01:12:07.220
What else?

1151
01:12:07.220 --> 01:12:08.220
It's your life.

1152
01:12:08.220 --> 01:12:09.220
It's a really, really long life.

1153
01:12:09.220 --> 01:12:10.220
Yeah, I see you.

1154
01:12:10.220 --> 01:12:11.220
Psychosis can do it.

1155
01:12:11.220 --> 01:12:13.220
With the sensory deprivation can cause it.

1156
01:12:13.220 --> 01:12:14.220
What else?

1157
01:12:14.220 --> 01:12:15.220
A UCI.

1158
01:12:15.220 --> 01:12:20.220
A UCI, an occult, hidden urinary tract infection in the elderly.

1159
01:12:20.220 --> 01:12:21.220
Classic.

1160
01:12:21.220 --> 01:12:28.220
Any occult infection, but the UTIs and the elderly are the major ones.

1161
01:12:28.220 --> 01:12:31.220
Have you heard of thyroid storm?

1162
01:12:31.220 --> 01:12:33.220
That's another one.

1163
01:12:33.220 --> 01:12:35.220
Have you heard of adrenal crisis?

1164
01:12:35.220 --> 01:12:41.220
That's another one.

1165
01:12:41.220 --> 01:12:48.220
So those are your, I even think roid rage, to some extent, could be classified.

1166
01:12:48.220 --> 01:12:52.220
Something like that, if they're actually seeing, hearing, hallucinating, and you know,

1167
01:12:52.220 --> 01:12:55.220
if they're that bad, it can be that.

1168
01:12:55.220 --> 01:12:58.220
Well, what's the good news for these people?

1169
01:12:58.220 --> 01:13:00.220
It's temporary.

1170
01:13:00.220 --> 01:13:01.220
It's temporary.

1171
01:13:01.220 --> 01:13:04.220
So really, what's the focus of managing these people?

1172
01:13:04.220 --> 01:13:10.220
Removing the underlying cause and keep them safe.

1173
01:13:10.220 --> 01:13:14.220
So what is the two steps?

1174
01:13:14.220 --> 01:13:15.220
Acknowledge what?

1175
01:13:15.220 --> 01:13:16.220
Feeling.

1176
01:13:16.220 --> 01:13:17.220
Feeling.

1177
01:13:17.220 --> 01:13:21.220
And then reassure.

1178
01:13:21.220 --> 01:13:22.220
Reassure.

1179
01:13:22.220 --> 01:13:27.220
Of what two things are you reassuring them?

1180
01:13:27.220 --> 01:13:32.220
That it's temporary, it will go away, and they will be kept safe.

1181
01:13:32.220 --> 01:13:37.220
So reassure them of safety, and that it will go away.

1182
01:13:37.220 --> 01:13:41.220
Why don't you present reality?

1183
01:13:41.220 --> 01:13:43.220
They're not going to get it.

1184
01:13:43.220 --> 01:13:44.220
They're not going to get it.

1185
01:13:44.220 --> 01:13:48.220
Why do you not redirect them?

1186
01:13:48.220 --> 01:13:49.220
Because it's not going to work.

1187
01:13:49.220 --> 01:13:52.220
It's just reassure them.

1188
01:13:52.220 --> 01:13:58.220
Let me give an example with all three.

1189
01:13:58.220 --> 01:14:05.220
Functional, dementia, delirium, and show you how the answer changes.

1190
01:14:05.220 --> 01:14:11.220
Because one of the things they do on boards is they change the type of psychosis,

1191
01:14:11.220 --> 01:14:15.220
and they look to see if you change your answer when they change the type of psychosis.

1192
01:14:15.220 --> 01:14:18.220
Or do you always go with present reality, present reality, present reality,

1193
01:14:18.220 --> 01:14:22.220
no matter who you're with, which is the wrong way to go.

1194
01:14:22.220 --> 01:14:24.220
Okay, here we go.

1195
01:14:24.220 --> 01:14:29.220
You have a patient with schizoaffective disorder who points to two people

1196
01:14:29.220 --> 01:14:32.220
talking at a table across the room.

1197
01:14:32.220 --> 01:14:33.220
Got the picture?

1198
01:14:33.220 --> 01:14:38.220
Schizoaffective, they're pointing to two people talking at a table across the room,

1199
01:14:38.220 --> 01:14:44.220
and they say, those people are plotting to kill me.

1200
01:14:44.220 --> 01:14:46.220
What would you say?

1201
01:14:46.220 --> 01:14:52.220
Well, what's the most important word in that whole question?

1202
01:14:52.220 --> 01:14:53.220
Schizoaffective.

1203
01:14:53.220 --> 01:14:56.220
Why is schizoaffective the most important word?

1204
01:14:56.220 --> 01:14:59.220
Because it throws them in the category of functional psychotic.

1205
01:14:59.220 --> 01:15:03.220
So what's the first thing you say to that person in that context?

1206
01:15:03.220 --> 01:15:07.220
Those two people over there are plotting to kill me.

1207
01:15:07.220 --> 01:15:09.220
I see you are frightened or scared of that.

1208
01:15:09.220 --> 01:15:10.220
It must be frightening.

1209
01:15:10.220 --> 01:15:15.220
Then what would you say?

1210
01:15:15.220 --> 01:15:16.220
Okay, but they don't say reality.

1211
01:15:16.220 --> 01:15:18.220
You got to know what it's going to look like.

1212
01:15:18.220 --> 01:15:22.220
What is it going to say?

1213
01:15:22.220 --> 01:15:25.220
Those people are not plotting to kill you.

1214
01:15:25.220 --> 01:15:27.220
We're all safe.

1215
01:15:27.220 --> 01:15:30.220
That's called presenting reality.

1216
01:15:30.220 --> 01:15:34.220
Then what do you say to that person?

1217
01:15:34.220 --> 01:15:37.220
Furthermore, we're not going to discuss this.

1218
01:15:37.220 --> 01:15:42.220
If they keep doing what he said, they keep talking about the two people over there.

1219
01:15:42.220 --> 01:15:46.220
I see you're too ill to have a conversation, so we're finished off to meet with you in a half an hour,

1220
01:15:46.220 --> 01:15:49.220
and then maybe you can have a reality-based conversation.

1221
01:15:49.220 --> 01:15:50.220
Those are all appropriate.

1222
01:15:50.220 --> 01:15:52.220
Do you see the four steps?

1223
01:15:52.220 --> 01:15:55.220
Okay, let me give you another question.

1224
01:15:55.220 --> 01:15:58.220
Are you a sister or a community member of that?

1225
01:15:58.220 --> 01:15:59.220
No, not necessarily.

1226
01:15:59.220 --> 01:16:01.220
No, not necessarily.

1227
01:16:01.220 --> 01:16:05.220
That is not the priority at that point in time.

1228
01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:08.220
The priority is presenting the reality, setting the limit.

1229
01:16:08.220 --> 01:16:11.220
Now, if they're escalating and they're starting to do it,

1230
01:16:11.220 --> 01:16:16.220
but there was no escalation in that question.

1231
01:16:16.220 --> 01:16:17.220
What about this?

1232
01:16:17.220 --> 01:16:21.220
You have a patient with Alzheimer's disease who, during your conversation,

1233
01:16:21.220 --> 01:16:25.220
points to two people sitting at a table and says,

1234
01:16:25.220 --> 01:16:28.220
you see those people, they're plotting to kill me.

1235
01:16:28.220 --> 01:16:31.220
