Think of narrow self-concept.

1482
01:30:21.220 --> 01:30:24.220
What would that mean?

1483
01:30:24.220 --> 01:30:26.220
What's that?

1484
01:30:26.220 --> 01:30:31.220
That would be low self-esteem.

1485
01:30:31.220 --> 01:30:36.220
This is narrowed self-concept.

1486
01:30:36.220 --> 01:30:40.220
Your self-concept is how you define who you are.

1487
01:30:40.220 --> 01:30:43.220
And how they define who they are is extremely narrow.

1488
01:30:43.220 --> 01:30:48.220
Meaning they define who they are based on what two things?

1489
01:30:48.220 --> 01:30:51.220
Where they are and what they're wearing.

1490
01:30:51.220 --> 01:30:56.220
So if you, the reason why they refuse to change their clothes and leave their room is

1491
01:30:56.220 --> 01:31:03.220
they don't know who they are unless they are wearing those things in that room.

1492
01:31:03.220 --> 01:31:06.220
And that's why they won't change it, because it's terrifying.

1493
01:31:06.220 --> 01:31:12.220
So as a nurse, would you make a functional psychotic who is refusing to leave their room and change their clothes,

1494
01:31:12.220 --> 01:31:14.220
would you make them do it?

1495
01:31:14.220 --> 01:31:17.220
Why?

1496
01:31:17.220 --> 01:31:21.220
If you made them do it, they would cease to know who they were.

1497
01:31:21.220 --> 01:31:25.220
You would have a panic, escalation. Somebody would get hurt.

1498
01:31:25.220 --> 01:31:27.220
So you do not do that.

1499
01:31:27.220 --> 01:31:31.220
So when do you, what do you tell them when they refuse to leave their room and change their clothes?

1500
01:31:31.220 --> 01:31:35.220
What do you tell a functional psychotic?

1501
01:31:35.220 --> 01:31:39.220
I see you are uncomfortable or upset.

1502
01:31:39.220 --> 01:31:46.220
You do not have to change your clothes or leave the room until you feel comfortable or until you're ready.

1503
01:31:46.220 --> 01:31:51.220
And it's a perfect world so they can stay there for 30 years if that's the case.

1504
01:31:51.220 --> 01:31:58.220
Don't say, well, they can't stay, that won't work because what if they're, what if it's three weeks and they still don't feel comfortable?

1505
01:31:58.220 --> 01:32:00.220
They won't be able to stay in the hospital that long.

1506
01:32:00.220 --> 01:32:06.220
Hey, it's a perfect world. Yes, they can stay that long.

1507
01:32:06.220 --> 01:32:09.220
However, what if they say this?

1508
01:32:09.220 --> 01:32:15.220
Mrs. Jones has been depressed since her last child went to college three months ago.

1509
01:32:15.220 --> 01:32:25.220
She has no energy to do the housework. She has lost her job because she didn't have the energy to get up and go to work.

1510
01:32:25.220 --> 01:32:34.220
She says, I hate the way I feel. I am going to kill myself and things don't get better because this depression is ruining my life. Please help me.

1511
01:32:34.220 --> 01:32:41.220
She is refusing to leave her room or eat her breakfast or do her daily hygiene care.

1512
01:32:41.220 --> 01:32:47.220
What do you do?

1513
01:32:47.220 --> 01:32:55.220
Acknowledge how she feels and then reinforce the reality that she is being.

1514
01:32:55.220 --> 01:33:01.220
Okay, but see, now you're taking the four step process before a psychotic. And this lady is a what?

1515
01:33:01.220 --> 01:33:10.220
She's a non-psychotic. She's a non-psychotic. She just said depression. She had insight, right?

1516
01:33:10.220 --> 01:33:13.220
So you don't have to read into it if it's major or not?

1517
01:33:13.220 --> 01:33:23.220
If they don't say major, it isn't major. They have to specify major.

1518
01:33:23.220 --> 01:33:31.220
So what do you use? Good therapeutic communication skills. But what do you say?

1519
01:33:31.220 --> 01:33:36.220
I see you are depressed and feeling down.

1520
01:33:36.220 --> 01:33:46.220
But what if a surgical patient is refusing to get out of bed after surgery? What do you do?

1521
01:33:46.220 --> 01:33:52.220
Do you say it's okay? They can stay in bed all day? What do you tell them? Do you get them up and out?

1522
01:33:52.220 --> 01:33:54.220
Maybe you'll feel better after you get them.

1523
01:33:54.220 --> 01:33:59.220
Yeah, but that's a guarantee. Maybe you'll feel better because they could walk and feel worse and now they don't trust you.

1524
01:33:59.220 --> 01:34:07.220
So what you would say is something like, it's time for you to shower, come with me, we will do it.

1525
01:34:07.220 --> 01:34:11.220
You're very what? Directive because she doesn't have the energy to do it.

1526
01:34:11.220 --> 01:34:12.220
Tell them.

1527
01:34:12.220 --> 01:34:18.220
What's that? Tell them. Yeah, just like you would a post-op who won't get out of bed.

1528
01:34:18.220 --> 01:34:22.220
Okay, now put your foot over the bed. Okay, now I'm going to raise you up. Now put your feet on the floor.

1529
01:34:22.220 --> 01:34:28.220
Now stand up. You're very directive because they're not what? Psychotic.

1530
01:34:29.220 --> 01:34:35.220
So what's my point? What's similar about those two situations I gave you and what's different?

1531
01:34:37.220 --> 01:34:42.220
The one was psychotic, had the narrowed self concept, was refusing to leave his room and change his clothes.

1532
01:34:42.220 --> 01:34:47.220
The other one was not psychotic, depressed, who was refusing to leave the room and change their clothes.

1533
01:34:47.220 --> 01:34:55.220
One, I told you what? Do not push them. Do not make them. With the depressed, what do you do?

1534
01:34:55.220 --> 01:35:00.220
You force them. You make them. If they don't make choices, you make choices for them.

1535
01:35:00.220 --> 01:35:08.220
You understand the only time you're allowed to make choices for patients is with depressed, psychomotally retarded patients.

1536
01:35:10.220 --> 01:35:17.220
So do you see the difference in the answers? Because your patient changed? Okay.

1537
01:35:18.220 --> 01:35:22.220
Ideas of reference, that's when you think everybody's talking about you.

1538
01:35:23.220 --> 01:35:29.220
For example, if two people were out there in the hall and they were talking, if I had ideas of reference, I'd think, what are they saying about me?

1539
01:35:32.220 --> 01:35:40.220
Now, psychotics have really, really taken this to the limit. I had one psychotic patient at Twin Valley where I took care of her.

1540
01:35:40.220 --> 01:35:49.220
And every evening when Katie Couric came on the news, she'd take her shoe off and throw it at the TV and say, call Katie, all kinds of choice names,

1541
01:35:50.220 --> 01:35:55.220
and say, why are you lying about me? I was never in New Mexico. I could not have done that.

1542
01:35:55.220 --> 01:36:04.220
What did she have? Ideas of reference because she thought Katie Couric was telling stories about her when it had absolutely nothing to do with her.

1543
01:36:04.220 --> 01:36:11.220
That's ideas of reference. Do you remember what I said about the guy, the people that pointed at the table and said, those people are plotting to kill me?

1544
01:36:11.220 --> 01:36:18.220
That's paranoia plus ideas of reference. And we dealt with it three different ways depending on who the patient was, correct?

1545
01:36:19.220 --> 01:36:26.220
Okay. Take a break. Come back at 10-0 and we'll do diabetes.
